<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Svapnavasavadatta</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as The Dream of Vasavadatta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Svapnavasavadatta, The Dream of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasavadatta">Vasavadatta</a>, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> play in six acts attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhasa">Bhasa</a>. It tells how King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udayana-of-vatsa">Udayana of Vatsa</a>, believing his queen Vasavadatta has died in a fire, is persuaded to marry a princess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> for political reasons, while Vasavadatta lives in disguise beside him. It is the best known of the plays recovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerala">Kerala</a> in 1912 and is still performed.</p></div>
